uid "cfa7a214-ba76-554e-beac-ba26ce0b267b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175556 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175556 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175556 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "556528e8-e14c-5170-a694-d9d52bcbb8de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175557 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175557 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175557 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7cda7705-15f5-55b4-9121-da930449a99b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175795 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6744" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175795 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (\\lambda-4)^6(\\lambda-9)^8,$ the rank of $(A-9I)^3$ equals $9,$ and the rank of $(A-9I)^4$ equals $7.$ If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 9?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175795 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13cf1317-c3a1-5175-9f5b-c3da13cad348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175795 #edb/ref 1074 "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda = 9,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\n(A - 9I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A - 9I)^3 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $14 \\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-9I)^3$ equals $9,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^3 = 14-9 = 5.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A - 9I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3$ of dimension $5$ in $\\mathbb{R}^{14}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-9I)^4$ equals $7,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^4 = 14-7 = 7.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A - 9I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$ of dimension $7$ in $\\mathbb{R}^{14}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3$ is a subspace of dimension $5$ in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$, which is a subspace of dimension $7,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$ but not in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^4 - \\text{nullity}(A-9I)^3 = 7-5=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175835 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9055" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175835 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (1+\\lambda) (4-\\lambda)^3(\\lambda-1)^5,$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1,$ there are exactly $3$ eigenvectors. If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175835 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "faa37d23-bdd2-5651-bf53-47835ee61cc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175835 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ equals the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda.$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = (1+\\lambda) (4-\\lambda)^3(\\lambda-1)^5,\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda = 1$ has multiplicity $5.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1$ is $5.$\n\nHowever, there are only $3$ eigenvectors corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1$ is $3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 1$ is $5-3=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175844 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9125" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175844 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (3+\\lambda)^3(\\lambda-\\pi)^8(\\lambda-2)^3,$ the nullity of $(A-\\pi I)^3$ equals $4,$ and the nullity of $(A-\\pi I)^4$ equals $6.$ If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = \\pi?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175844 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bed5f45f-5c76-542b-965f-eadf474823c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175844 #edb/ref 1074 "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda = \\pi,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\n(A - \\pi I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A - \\pi I)^3 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^3$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(A - \\pi I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^4$ has dimension $6.$ This means that the solutions to $(A - \\pi I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $6.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^3$ is a subspace of dimension $4$ in $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^4$, which is a subspace of dimension $6,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^4$ but not in $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^3.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-\\pi I)^4 - \\text{nullity}(A-\\pi I)^3 = 6-4=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=\\pi.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175946 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175946 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175946 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175946 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175946 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175946 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175946 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175946 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175946 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175946 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175946 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175946 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2dbed47b-77e4-53fd-8eb9-17ec13e42a3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175984 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175984 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175984 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175984 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175984 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175984 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175984 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175984 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175984 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175984 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186386452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175984 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175984 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank Greater Than One" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175984 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175984 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "dfc9161a-5f14-5c16-afb0-832f81f30be4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175993 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175993 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175993 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175993 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175993 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175993 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186375727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175993 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175993 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175993 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank Using Nullity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175993 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175993 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "85ecbb00-cabe-5838-9ac4-50ebc8821c05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176150 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176150 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176150 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176150 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176150 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176150 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176150 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176150 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176150 #edb/ref 1045 "Ranks of Generalized Eigenvectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176150 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176150 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176150 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176150 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "eaad5957-8a77-5c3e-bc8d-81b1c2325346" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176247 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176247 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109740" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176247 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176247 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{3}(4-\\lambda )^{2}(7-\\lambda )^{6},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D,$ how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176247 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3cb467c0-ea77-596f-b372-4352d634576f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176247 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{3}(4-\\lambda )^{2}(7-\\lambda )^{6},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =0$ has multiplicity $3.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $3.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =0$ is $3-2=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176251 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176251 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176251 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176251 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}(\\lambda +1)^{16}(\\lambda -8)^{2},$ the nullity of $(A+I)^{4}$ equals $4,$ and the nullity of $(A+I)^{5}$ equals $7.$ If a basis of ${R}^{20}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176251 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27ab5139-4b6b-5639-b90d-136b8a14f7bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176251 #edb/ref 1074 "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $5$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A+I)^{5}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A+I)^{4}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{4}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(A+I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}$ has dimension $7.$ This means that the solutions to $(A+I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $7.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{4}$ is a subspace of dimension $4$ in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}$, which is a subspace of dimension $7,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+I)^{5}-\\text{nullity}(A+I)^{4}=7-4=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176569 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176569 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176569 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176569 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=\\lambda (\\lambda +1)^{3}(\\lambda -2)^{4},$ the rank of $(A-2I)^{2}$ equals $7,$ and the rank of $(A-2I)^{3}$ equals $6.$ If a basis of ${R}^{8}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176569 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e04a0ca0-a400-5c37-86e4-06c47bca3608" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176569 #edb/ref 1074 "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-2I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be an $8\\times 8$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-2I)^{2}$ equals $7,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{2}=8-7=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-2I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{2}$ of dimension $1$ in ${R}^{8}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-2I)^{3}$ equals $6,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{3}=8-6=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-2I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{3}$ of dimension $2$ in ${R}^{8}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $1$ in $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $2,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{2}=2-1=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176672 #edb/ref 1061 24945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176672 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "78c5549c-e0d5-53d1-a816-9e9092586d36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176672 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176729 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176729 #edb/ref 1061 33796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176729 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3961ed3f-bdbb-58d8-946c-67fb1150da8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176729 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176730 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176730 #edb/ref 1061 33797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176730 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fb412f74-1e1a-5c76-a91c-35d89b5cd5f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176730 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176731 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176731 #edb/ref 1061 33798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176731 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cbffc464-3ec8-51a7-bad6-e40d2c96d1fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176731 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176743 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176743 #edb/ref 1061 34012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176743 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "32f06230-5f13-50e6-adb6-a7143bdba331" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176743 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176835 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176835 #edb/ref 1061 37212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176835 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8ab2409-9530-5042-8db2-6e6b344401ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176835 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176836 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176836 #edb/ref 1061 37213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176836 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5899fe18-80e2-5145-909a-f3f95dbb69db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176836 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176994 #edb/ref 1064 "For some $n\\times n$ matrices, it's possible to construct a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ consisting entirely of linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas eigenvalues $\\lambda_1 = 5, \\lambda_2 = 4,$ and $\\lambda_3 = 2,$ with corresponding linearly independent eigenvectors \n\n![](images/45/451c66729d04278ec42f1ffcc558245b5cd2ef14b0c3d14dbd35447702b7b873.svg)\nThese eigenvectors form a basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nHowever, there exist $n\\times n$ matrices whose eigenvectors do *not* form a basis of $\\mathbb R^n.$ \n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n![](images/65/653166c6712102e8afacb5e2a73de617a3777e6812d9abbeb760f72b664525b3.svg)\n\nhas the single eigenvalue ${\\color{black}\\lambda=4}$ of algebraic multiplicity $3.$ However, there are only two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, namely\n\n![](images/50/5007b5e49662ccb6aad508b233f4d107bdb89afc2607eee76280a16ec02d6f90.svg)\n\nSince there are only two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we say that the **geometric multiplicity** of ${\\color{black}\\lambda=4}$ equals  $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176994 #edb/ref 1065 5420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176994 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "931f2f11-a4be-5b57-83cc-47ca54fc5443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176994 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176995 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a generalized eigenvector of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of  $A$ is a nonzero vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ In other words, $A-\\lambda I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $n$ steps. This is equivalent to the following:\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)^n \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad\n(A - \\lambda I)^{n-1} \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nBy the definition of the null space of a matrix, $(A - \\lambda I)^k \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ if and only if $\\mathbf{v} \\in \\text{Null}(A-\\lambda I)^k.$\nThis, in turn, means that a generalized eigenvector of rank $n$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A-\\lambda I)^n$ but not in $\\text{Null}(A-\\lambda I)^{n-1}.$ \n\n![](images/8b/8b0e00c74440c269a1d1b1c076aad56dfe3a056a1493c28862e4d080b5c33563.png)\n\nFor example, suppose that a matrix $A$ has the characteristic polynomial \n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = (5-\\lambda)^6(\\lambda-3)^2(\\lambda+4)^5.\n$$\n\nIn addition, let's assume the following:\n\n- The nullity of $(A+4I)^3$ equals $2.$\n\n- The nullity of $(A+4I)^4$ equals $3.$\n\nIf a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{13}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = -4?$\n\nFor the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda = -4,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\n(A + 4I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad\n(A + 4I)^3 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^3$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(A +4I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^4$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(A +4I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^3$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^4$, which is a subspace of dimension $3,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^4$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^3.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)^4 - \\text{nullity}(A+4I)^3 = 3-2=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-4.$\n\nIn general, we have the following theorem:\n\n*The number of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is equal to \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-\\lambda I)^n - \\text{nullity}(A-\\lambda I)^{n-1}.\n$$\n\n*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176995 #edb/ref 1065 5421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176995 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e3504196-e96f-544f-89f9-a985b82f8ddb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176995 #edb/ref 1067 "The Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank for a Given Eigenvalue" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177052 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has the characteristic polynomial \n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\lambda^4 (1-\\lambda)^3(1+\\lambda)^7,\n$$\n\nand that there are precisely $4$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda = -1.$ \n\nIf a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = -1?$\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- In the characteristic polynomial, the root $\\lambda =-1$ has multiplicity $7.$ So, the *algebraic* multiplicity of $\\lambda = -1$ is $7.$\n\n- However, we're told that there are only $4$ eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda = -1.$ So, the *geometric* multiplicity of $\\lambda = -1$ is $4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda = -1$ is \n\n$$\n7-4=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177052 #edb/ref 1065 6126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177052 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ccaaceee-ceed-5a3f-be84-18703a3e27a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177052 #edb/ref 1067 "A Second Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177053 #edb/ref 1064 "So, not every $n\\times n$ matrix has the property that its eigenvectors form a basis of $\\mathbb R^n.$\n\nDespite this, we have the following theorem:\n\n*For any real $n \\times n$ matrix, there is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ consisting of generalized eigenvectors of the matrix.*\n\nTo illustrate, let's go back to our matrix \n![](images/d0/d09805a20bbc23954fdec5a893e05ec0bad45a6503e27354df60b4d71952f990.svg)\n\nwhere the eigenvectors corresponding to the single eigenvalue $\\lambda = 4$ are\n\n![](images/e6/e6053d03e5e0f4bc843856d88dbc9e8e14252200a5d7280a4a05872b19bd4d56.svg)\n\nRemember that $\\lambda = 4$ has *algebraic* multiplicity $3$ and *geometric* multiplicity $2.$\n\nNow, it's easy to show that $A$ has a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ given by\n\n![](images/89/890d3b69aa88f0f442760d03afc7333d2e918b5b3401a33b167cfa40321a5f4c.svg)\nand our generalized eigenvectors form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nClearly, ${\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{u}}}, {\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{v}}},$ and $\\color{red}\\mathbf{w}$ form a basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ In particular, our basis consists of\n\n- two eigenvectors ($\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}$), and\n\n- one generalized eigenvector whose rank is greater than one ($\\color{red}\\mathbf{w}$).\n\nNotice that the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{algebraic multiplicity}-\\text{geometric multiplicity} & =3-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is no coincidence. In general, we have the following theorem:\n\n*The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ equals the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177053 #edb/ref 1065 6127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177053 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ad92ba79-e1b7-5818-9d69-6fd643580cdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177053 #edb/ref 1067 "Forming a Basis Using Generalized Eigenvectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362724 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362724 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362724 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186362725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362724 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bd562744-db8f-593a-9c8b-77f2c75d981e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362725 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362725 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362725 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f99e9009-e6c2-582d-90ae-d0bfcf1f5d58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362726 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362726 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362726 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "99d135b5-1334-5d73-b629-c04ae8d41c33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362727 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362727 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362727 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2dac6776-db44-5e53-8d0e-7467411d2060" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362728 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362728 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362728 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ac89cd1b-b62b-50dd-b0a5-0452ce5ed0f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362729 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362729 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362729 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "34ffef47-4e58-50ef-b079-896389f7f959" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364420 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364420 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364420 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364420 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(1+\\lambda )^{4}(1-\\lambda )^{2}(4-\\lambda )^{3},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ there is exactly $1$ eigenvector. If a basis of ${R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364420 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "246e62ce-be4d-58bc-8aa3-d8a9e5b8b661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364420 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(1+\\lambda )^{4}(1-\\lambda )^{2}(4-\\lambda )^{3},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =-1$ has multiplicity $4.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $4.$\n\nHowever, there is only $1$ eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $1.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1$ is $4-1=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364421 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364421 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364421 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364421 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364421 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364421 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364421 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364421 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186364422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364421 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f7b1e17b-e37b-5c86-92c1-41321a65eb42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364422 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364422 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364422 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e865e05-d063-5259-9427-8e9ac3415420" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364423 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364423 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364423 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e4d54eb7-8c94-5ba1-8ff1-0e33e8e8ff4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364424 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364424 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364424 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2ca18b90-c676-50ef-bb00-24c4e41c4b25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364425 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364425 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364425 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "61161b93-ea6b-5325-aa7b-d6e1119b0a59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364426 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364426 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364426 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1c60cb57-ff4f-5029-8ce2-b8fe29a39c9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375720 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375720 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375720 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375720 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $S$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(7+\\lambda )^{3}(\\lambda -\\sqrt{3})^{4}(\\lambda +2)^{3},$ the nullity of $(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}$ equals $3,$ and the nullity of $(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}$ equals $4.$ If a basis of ${R}^{10}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $S,$ how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375720 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fab609bc-c26d-54d9-ae3c-82ed6883e1ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375720 #edb/ref 1074 "For the given matrix $S$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3},$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $3$ in $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $4,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}=4-3=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375721 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375721 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375721 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186375724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375721 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3538ec67-039b-5162-a8f1-3e30a944e77d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375722 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375722 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375722 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f71f7292-bd10-52